# .mailmap - one canonical identity per contributor
#
# Why this file exists
# --------------------
# This repository is research data for a PhD on human oversight in agentic
# software development. Counting "who authored what" only means something if
# each contributor appears as exactly one identity. Git records whatever name
# and email were configured at commit time, so the same person drifts into
# several spellings over the life of a repository (a work laptop, a rebuilt
# machine, a GitHub noreply address). This file collapses those variants.
#
# It only changes DISPLAY. Git reads it when it shows history - `git log`,
# `git shortlog`, `git blame`, `git check-mailmap`. The commit objects
# themselves are never touched and no history is rewritten. Deleting this file
# would restore the raw names; nothing else would change.
#
# It applies to the CAPITAL format placeholders only: `%aN`/`%aE` and
# `%cN`/`%cE` are mailmap-aware, while `%an`/`%ae` and `%cn`/`%ce` print the
# raw, unmerged values. Anything that counts contributors must use the
# capitals, or it will count the variants separately and this file will have
# done nothing.
#
# The human/AI distinction is the data - keep it
# ----------------------------------------------
# Claude is credited through `Co-authored-by:` trailers and is kept as its own
# canonical identity. Do NOT map it onto the human author: which work was
# co-authored by a model, and which was not, is precisely what is being
# measured here.
#
# Format
# ------
#   Canonical Name <canonical@email>                         # set name for an email
#   Canonical Name <canonical@email> <other@email>           # merge another email
#   Canonical Name <canonical@email> Old Name <other@email>  # merge a name+email pair
#
# Adding a variant later
# ----------------------
# When `git shortlog -sne --all` (authors and committers) or
# `git shortlog -sne --group=trailer:co-authored-by --all` (co-authors) shows a
# spelling that is not listed below, add one line under the right heading: the
# canonical identity first, then the variant exactly as git printed it. Then
# re-run both commands - the variant should have disappeared into its canonical
# row - and confirm with `git check-mailmap "Old Name <other@email>"`.


# --- Human author ------------------------------------------------------------
# Canonical human identity. The bare line fixes the display name for this
# address, so a future `user.name` change on another machine still shows as
# `srimalhs` without needing a new line here.
srimalhs <srimalhs@gmail.com>


# --- AI co-author ------------------------------------------------------------
# Claude is credited per commit via `Co-authored-by:`. Anthropic's trailer
# carries the model name of the day ("Claude Opus 5 (1M context)"), which would
# otherwise split into a new identity with every model release. The bare line
# below normalises every name used with Anthropic's noreply address to the
# single name `Claude`, so present and future model names collapse with no
# further edits here. Confirm with:
#   git check-mailmap "Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>"
#
# A variant at a DIFFERENT address is the only case needing a new line, and it
# takes the merge form - canonical identity first, then the variant:
#   Claude <noreply@anthropic.com> <other@address>
Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
